From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: kernel-hive <wnt@example.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 19:06:19 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] kbd: return from the check-keystroke functions with
 interrupts enabled

The IBM AT BIOS executes STI on entry to INT 16h and returns from the
"check keystroke" functions (AH=01h/11h) via RET 2, so the caller always
gets IF=1 back regardless of the IF it pushed.  DOS resident software
relies on that: MS-DOS 6 POWER.EXE chains INT 16h with "pushf; call far"
while IF is still clear (it was itself entered through INT), then returns
to its own caller with RETF 2 to hand back ZF, which also hands back IF.

SeaBIOS returns via iretw with the pushed IF, so through such a chain the
caller ends up with interrupts disabled.  Under Windows for Workgroups
3.11 (386 enhanced mode) that IF=0 is copied back into the protected-mode
System VM after every keyboard-driver INT 16h poll and, when the running
app never yields, the guest runs with IF clear until reset (kernel-hive:
the win311 station freeze).

Set F_IF for the non-blocking dequeue, matching the AT BIOS contract.
---
 src/kbd.c | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/kbd.c b/src/kbd.c
index 15e5ae7..9e8d5fc 100644
--- a/src/kbd.c
+++ b/src/kbd.c
@@ -55,6 +55,16 @@ static void
 dequeue_key(struct bregs *regs, int incr, int extended)
 {
     yield();
+    // The IBM AT BIOS executes STI on entry and returns from the
+    // "check keystroke" functions (01h/11h) via RET 2, so the caller
+    // always gets IF=1 back regardless of the IF it pushed.  DOS
+    // resident software (e.g. MS-DOS 6 POWER.EXE) chains INT 16h with
+    // "pushf; call far" while IF is clear and then returns to its own
+    // caller with RETF 2, relying on this to re-enable interrupts.
+    // Returning the pushed IF instead leaves such callers with
+    // interrupts disabled for good (kernel-hive: WfW 3.11 wedge).
+    if (!incr)
+        regs->flags |= F_IF;
     u16 buffer_head;
     u16 buffer_tail;
     for (;;) {
-- 
